`Plugin.View()` is dispatched via `Dispatcher.BeginInvoke` on the WPF UI thread. The blocking COM chain — `Activator.CreateInstance` → `Initialize` → `DoPreview` — was freezing the entire UI for 7–8 seconds on first use while the Office preview handler (typically `prevhost.exe`) cold-started.

## Changes

- **`PreviewHandlerHost`** — adds `OpenBackground(path, parentHandle, clientRect)`: accepts pre-captured HWND and client rect so it can be called from a background thread without touching thread-affine WinForms `Control` properties. `CurrentPreviewHandler` is now assigned after successful initialization to prevent `OnResize` from observing a partially-initialized handler.

- **`PreviewPanel`** — `PreviewFile()` captures `Handle` and `ClientRectangle` on the UI thread, then hands off all blocking COM work to a **dedicated STA background thread**. A `CancellationTokenSource` ensures that rapid file switching or `Dispose()` cannot deliver a stale `IsBusy = false` to the new preview context.

- **`Plugin.View()`** — removes the unconditional `context.IsBusy = false` from the UI thread; it is now set by the background thread on completion, or in the `catch` block on error.

```csharp
// Before: DoPreview() blocks UI thread for 7-8s on first call
_panel.PreviewFile(path, context);  // synchronous COM chain on dispatcher thread
context.IsBusy = false;

// After: UI thread returns immediately; STA thread signals completion
var hwnd = _control.Handle;         // captured on UI thread
var rect = _control.ClientRectangle;
var thread = new Thread(() => {
    _control.OpenBackground(file, hwnd, rect);  // blocks on background STA thread
    if (!cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        context.IsBusy = false;
}) { IsBackground = true };
thread.SetApartmentState(ApartmentState.STA);
thread.Start();
```

